-- erwartet: K003 allein
-- **1057 -- a caller promising costs over a costless `extern fn`.**
--
-- A `costs <= N ops` clause on an `extern fn` is TRUSTED: the checker counts
-- the number, it does not re-measure the foreign body. Where the clause is
-- missing there is nothing to count, so a caller that promises costs meets
-- `K003` over the edge -- the promise would otherwise be a number over an
-- unknown quantity. Missing costs stays `K003`; no weaker reading is built.
--
-- `-- erwartet: K003 allein` is the sharper claim and it is the right one
-- here: without this rule NOTHING falls -- the unit checks clean and the
-- emitted C is valid `cc -Werror` input (measured on the costed twin, which
-- is gift 1058 at a promise of 7). That is the counterfactual the trust
-- needs: the rule is the only line between the promise and the shipment.
--
-- Nothing else speaks on purpose: both sides promise `pure` over `pure`
-- (`E008` silent), the call is well-typed (`M1` silent), and the callee is
-- called once, so no second `K003` stands beside the edge fault. The older
-- `K003` probe is gift 36 (same code, module-less style, not `allein`); the
-- declaration-side analogue on the `syscall` edge is gift 986 (`N322`).

module gift::extern_ohne_kosten {

extern fn stumm() -> u32 effects { pure };

impl fn ruft() -> u32
    effects { pure }
    costs <= 7 ops
{
    let x = stumm();
    return x;
}

}
